:beauty
~/psalms/19 3:1 ~/psalms/23 {Psalm 19 is two masterpieces fused: the silent speech of the heavens (vv1-6), then the law as the sweetest thing (vv7-11). The shepherd psalm is perfect but singular. 19 does two things.}
~/psalms/22 3:1 ~/psalms/51 {Both are anguish but 22 has greater range — the opening cry, the worm passage, the bulls of Bashan, the sudden turn to praise. 51 is purer confession but narrower.}
~/psalms/131 3:1 ~/psalms/133 {Psalm 131 is three verses of perfect compression: I do not occupy myself with things too great. The weaned child image is untranslatable. 133 is beautiful but more conventional.}
~/psalms/73 3:1 ~/psalms/37 {73 is a psychological drama — Asaph's envy of the wicked, his near fall, his resolution in the sanctuary. 37 covers similar ground more flatly.}
~/psalms/90 3:1 ~/psalms/46 {A thousand years in thy sight are but as a watch in the night. 90 is Moses confronting eternity and it sustains that confrontation. 46 has the great still-and-know line but is less complete.}
~/psalms/139 3:1 ~/psalms/103 {139 is more intimate and more strange — the psalmist cannot escape God, the darkness is as light, search me and know me. 103 is magnificent gratitude but less piercing.}
~/psalms/8 3:1 ~/psalms/29 {What is man that thou art mindful of him — the question that makes the praise more devastating. 29 lists the voice of the LORD with power but 8 has the better question.}
~/psalms/137 3:1 ~/psalms/130 {137 contains the most beautiful opening in all the psalms and then the most violent ending — that tension is a literary achievement. 130 is a great cry but more uniform.}
~/psalms/104 3:1 ~/psalms/148 {104 is the great creation meditation — Leviathan playing in the sea, the stork in the fir trees, man going to his labor. 148 is also a creation praise but a list rather than a poem.}
~/psalms/88 3:1 ~/psalms/102 {88 has the most distinctive voice of any lament — the darkness is my closest friend. 102 is also a dark lament but it turns toward hope. 88 refuses the turn.}
~/psalms/23 3:1 ~/psalms/121 {Both are pastoral and comforting but 23 has richer imagery and the valley of the shadow as its center. 121 is more hymnic.}
~/psalms/84 3:1 ~/psalms/42 {How lovely is thy dwelling place — 84 opens with longing for the temple courts and sustains it. 42 has the deer panting but also more complaint.}

:depth
~/psalms/139 3:1 ~/psalms/23 {139 contains more that resists easy resolution: where shall I flee from thy presence? the darkness is as light. The shepherd psalm is deep but more immediately received.}
~/psalms/73 3:1 ~/psalms/49 {73 is the theodicy psalm — the crisis of faith when the wicked prosper, resolved only when Asaph enters the sanctuary. 49 covers similar ground (wealth at death) but more abstractly.}
~/psalms/90 3:1 ~/psalms/8 {90 confronts time and mortality with more weight: we spend our years as a tale that is told. 8 asks the great question but is briefer and less developed.}
~/psalms/22 3:1 ~/psalms/88 {22 has the deepest structure — the utter abandonment of the opening, the historical memory of the fathers, the return to praise — a complete arc of faith through despair. 88 is deep but has only one direction.}
~/psalms/119 3:1 ~/psalms/1 {119 is a meditation that goes around the law 22 times, exploring it from every angle. 1 opens that meditation perfectly but briefly. 119 is the deeper document.}
~/psalms/82 3:1 ~/psalms/50 {82 places God in the divine council judging the gods: ye are gods but ye shall die like men. That cosmological frame is stranger and deeper than 50's critique of sacrifice.}
~/psalms/89 3:1 ~/psalms/44 {89 starts with the covenant with David — I will not lie unto David — and then the covenant seems to have collapsed. The theological crisis it names is acute. 44 is also accusatory but less specific in what it accuses God of failing.}
~/psalms/19 3:1 ~/psalms/119 {Psalm 19 contains the insight that 119 elaborates: the law is sweeter than honey, by it is thy servant warned. 19 compresses the insight; 119 deepens it through repetition.}

:comfort
~/psalms/23 5:1 ~/psalms/91 {The LORD is my shepherd — even the valley of the shadow becomes traversable. The shepherd carries. 91 is more about protection than companionship.}
~/psalms/121 3:1 ~/psalms/46 {I will lift up mine eyes to the hills. The LORD shall preserve thy going out and coming in. 121 is pure comfort without the trouble of 46. 46 comforts through power; 121 through presence.}
~/psalms/103 3:1 ~/psalms/34 {As far as the east is from the west, so far hath he removed our transgressions. 103 is the most complete statement of forgiveness in the psalms. 34 is comfort mixed with instruction.}
~/psalms/131 3:1 ~/psalms/127 {My soul is even as a weaned child — the image of rest after wanting is more comforting than unless the LORD builds, which is conditional.}
~/psalms/23 3:1 ~/psalms/16 {Both are psalms of trust but 23 has the concrete imagery — the table, the cup, the anointed head. 16 is more abstract in its contentment.}
~/psalms/91 3:1 ~/psalms/27 {He shall cover thee with his feathers — 91 is specifically about shelter and protection. 27 has the great the LORD is my light and salvation but also more complaint and more uncertainty.}

:strangeness
~/psalms/88 5:1 ~/psalms/22 {88 ends without resolution: darkness is my closest friend. No other psalm does this. 22 has the desperate opening but turns to praise. 88 refuses to turn.}
~/psalms/82 4:1 ~/psalms/89 {God standeth in the congregation of the mighty and judgeth among the gods. The premise is polytheistic and the ending — gods will die like men — is extraordinary. 89 names a covenant failure but stays within normal theology.}
~/psalms/137 3:1 ~/psalms/88 {Happy shall he be that taketh and dasheth thy little ones against the stones — that ending after the beautiful weeping psalm is the most violently strange moment in the psalter. 88 is strangely hopeless; 137 is strangely violent.}
~/psalms/44 3:1 ~/psalms/74 {44 accuses God directly: thou hast cast us off and put us to shame. Thou hast sold thy people for nought. The directness of the accusation is strange and uncomfortable. 74 is also accusatory but more measured.}
~/psalms/49 3:1 ~/psalms/73 {Man that is in honour and understandeth not is like the beasts that perish — the qoheleth-like refrain is stranger than 73's theodicy because it applies equally to the wise.}
~/psalms/39 3:1 ~/psalms/90 {I was dumb with silence, I held my peace even from good — 39 has a strange self-silencing quality, asking God to look away. 90 confronts mortality more directly.}

:challenge
~/psalms/15 3:1 ~/psalms/24 {Who shall abide in thy tabernacle? — the answer is a demanding ethical list. 24 asks the same question but answers it more briefly. 15 lingers in the requirement.}
~/psalms/50 3:1 ~/psalms/51 {I will not reprove thee for thy sacrifices — 50 says God does not need the burnt offerings, he wants thanksgiving and paying vows. 51 is the response: a broken and contrite heart. 50 issues the challenge; 51 is the response to it.}
~/psalms/139 3:1 ~/psalms/19 {Search me, O God, and know my heart — 139 invites the very examination it describes. 19 also ends with cleanse thou me from secret faults but as a brief coda rather than the whole poem.}
~/psalms/1 3:1 ~/psalms/37 {Psalm 1 demands a clear binary — the way of the righteous versus the way of the ungodly. 37 covers the same ground but more gradually and more pastorally.}
~/psalms/82 3:1 ~/psalms/15 {Defend the poor and fatherless: do justice to the afflicted and needy — 82 issues this as divine command in the council of the gods. The source makes it more challenging than 15's human inventory.}
